# ---- piska 123 (19:1-4): the section's shape; the statute and the vestments; the treasury; the heifer's age, color, blemish and yoke; Eleazar the adjutant; the hand, the finger, the seven sprinklings ----
srow(123, 1, 'MATERIAL', 'THE SECTION GENERAL-THEN-PARTICULAR; "STATUTE" HERE AND AT YOM KIPPUR — THE WHITE VESTMENTS (R. ELIEZER) AGAINST R. YOCHANAN B. ZAKKAI\'S GOLDEN; FROM THE TEMPLE TREASURY; "UNTO YOU" — MOSES AND AARON APPOINTED OVER IT; THE HEIFER\'S AGE BY THE WORD (EGLAH / PARAH); RED, WHOLE IN REDNESS; THE BLEMISH CLAUSE NEEDED AGAINST THE A-FORTIORI\'S REFUTATIONS; ISSI B. AKIVA — THE EGLAH ARUFAH EXCLUDED; R. YEHUDAH B. BETEIRA — A BLEMISH THAT PASSED; THE YOKE NOT IN WORKING-TIME, AND OTHER LABORS EQUATED WITH IT BY THE A-FORTIORI, TRANSPOSED, REFUTED AND RETURNED. "This is the statute of the Torah" (19:2) — a section GENERAL at the head ("the statute of the Torah") and PARTICULAR after ("a red heifer, whole") — with Exod 19:3-6 (particular then general) and 12:43 (general then particular) as the three shapes: THE RULE — the general contains only what the particular holds (I4-I5 named on the chapter\'s own opening; the ink: "this is the statute of the Torah" at 19:2 and 31:21 alone, computed); R. ELIEZER: "statute" here and "statute" at Yom Kippur (Lev 16:34) — as there in the WHITE vestments, so here (I2); R. YOCHANAN B. ZAKKAI to his disciples: "in the GOLDEN vestments" — "but did you not teach us the white?" — "if I have forgotten what my eyes saw and my hands ministered, how much more what I taught!" — said to sharpen them (others: Hillel, who as no priest could not say "my hands"): THE TEACHER\'S DELIBERATE ERROR recorded as a teaching device; "and let them take" — from the Temple treasury; "unto you" — that you be appointed over it, and Aaron with Moses (the oil for lighting\'s "unto you", Exod 27:20, the same rule); "a heifer (parah)": R. Eliezer — "eglah" a yearling, "parah" a two-year-old; the sages — eglah two, parah three or four; R. Meir — five valid too; an old one valid but not awaited lest black hairs sprout (the heifer\'s AGE a parameter with three recorded settings — the data channel; the ink: the heifer-noun\'s Torah seats all in this chapter, computed); "red" against black or white; "whole" — in redness (the blemish clause accounting for blemishes: I13\'s form, the two words each given a job); "which has no blemish" — why written? the a-fortiori from offerings (not invalidated by work, invalidated by a blemish — the heifer, invalidated by work, the more) REFUTED (offerings processed in cleanness; the heifer by a tvul yom), the refutation ANSWERED by the Paschal lamb (processed in tumah, invalidated by a blemish), and that answer REFUTED (the lamb has a fixed time) — so the verse is needed: THE A-FORTIORI RUN TO ITS FAILURE and the clause kept as the source (the protocol recorded in full — MIDDOT.md); ISSI B. AKIVA: the a-fortiori from black-and-white hairs holds, so the clause EXCLUDES the eglah arufah from blemish-invalidation (Deut 21:4 — the ink: the eglah\'s yoke clause "has not DRAWN in a yoke" a different verb from the heifer\'s "no yoke has COME upon her", which the Philistines\' ark-cows share, 1 Sam 6:7 — computed); R. YEHUDAH B. BETEIRA: from the bird sin-offering (its offerers clean, no blemish invalidates) the heifer the more should be valid — the clause needed; and "WHEN there is no blemish IN it" — a blemish that PASSED leaves it valid (R. Yoshiyah Numithi shown "between two fingers" — protruding flesh, two tails); "upon which a yoke has not come" — a yoke NOT in working-time; in working-time by the a-fortiori from the eglah arufah, REFUTED by offerings, the refutation answered by black-and-white; OTHER LABORS equated with a yoke by the a-fortiori from the eglah arufah ("which has never been WORKED", Deut 21:3), TRANSPOSED (the heifer\'s labors not equated, so the eglah\'s the less), the transposition REFUTED by "which has never been worked" — "I have reasoned a fortiori and I have transposed; the transposition has been refuted and I return to the original": THE PROTOCOL\'S FORMULA verbatim (I1 with reversal and refutation; Mishnah Parah 1-2\'s rows the answer sheet — the age, the hairs, the yoke). The exam: Mishnah Parah 1:1 (the age), 2:1-5 (red, the hairs, the yoke, the blemish); Yoma 42b-43b (the vestments; the adjutant); Chullin 11a (the a-fortiori from the heifer); Sotah 46a (the eglah\'s yoke).')
srow(123, 2, 'MATERIAL', 'ELEAZAR — THE ADJUTANT HIGH PRIEST (AARON ALIVE); THIS ONE BY ELEAZAR, LATER ONES BY THE HIGH PRIEST (MEIR) OR ANY PRIEST (THE FOUR); "HE SHALL TAKE IT OUT" ALONE — NO BLACK ONE WITH IT; THE MOUNT OF ANOINTMENT; CARRION AT SLAUGHTER UNFIT; NOT TWO TOGETHER; ANOTHER SLAUGHTERS, ELEAZAR LOOKS ON — WORK INVALIDATES THE SLAUGHTER; "THE PRIEST" IN HIS VESTMENTS; THE HAND, NOT A VESSEL — AGAINST THE LEPER\'S LOG AND HIS GUILT-OFFERING; THE RIGHT INDEX FINGER FROM LEV 14:16; THE GAZE AT THE SANCTUARY\'S DOOR; NO SANCTUARY, NO HEIFER; SEVEN DIPPINGS, MUTUALLY INCLUSIVE BY THE INNER SPRINKLINGS. "And you shall give it to Eleazar the priest" (19:3): processed by the ADJUTANT high priest — Aaron alive, Eleazar burned it (the ink: Eleazar at 19:3-4 and again at 20:25-28 taking Aaron\'s garments — the heifer\'s priest becomes the vested priest inside the portion, computed); R. MEIR: this one by Eleazar, the later ones by the high priest; R. YOSSI, R. YEHUDAH, R. SHIMON, R. ELAZAR B. YAAKOV: later ones by the high priest OR a common priest — a dispute on the officiant\'s rank (Mishnah Parah 4:1); "and he shall take it out" — ALONE: no black heifer led out with it lest they say the black was slaughtered (R. Yossi: from "it" alone); "outside the camp" — to the MOUNT OF ANOINTMENT (the Mount of Olives, its blood sprinkled toward the sanctuary\'s door); "and he shall slaughter it" — carrion at slaughter unfit; not two with one knife; "before him" — ANOTHER slaughters and Eleazar LOOKS ON, and WORK invalidates the slaughter (the ink: "and he shall slaughter it before him" one seat, computed); "and Eleazar the priest shall take" — why repeat "the priest"? in his priesthood — the vestments; "of its blood with his finger" — A MITZVAH OF THE HAND, not of a vessel: the a-fortiori from the leper\'s log (kasher only by the hand; both take seven sprinklings) against the leper\'s guilt-offering (from a vessel to the hand) — the verse decides ("with his finger": the ink\'s finger-word at the sin-offering\'s, Yom Kippur\'s and the leper\'s seats — nine, computed); "with his finger" — the RIGHT INDEX finger: Lev 14:16 specifies "his right finger" once, and all Torah "fingers" are the right\'s most skilful (I3, the leper\'s finger the paradigm); "opposite the tent of meeting" — his GAZE toward the sanctuary\'s door; if the sanctuary were not set up or the wind furled the curtains — NO HEIFER (the rite conditioned on the standing tent: the compile\'s condition); "of its blood seven times" — he RETURNS to the blood seven times, not seven from one dipping; the seven MUTUALLY INCLUSIVE — the a-fortiori from the inner sprinklings refuted (they atone) and the identity "seven times" / "seven times before the LORD" (Lev 16:14) deciding (I2). The exam: Mishnah Parah 3 (the procession, the mount, the slaughter, the sprinkling, the burning); 4:1 (the rank); Zevachim 93a-b; Yoma 43a-b (the finger, the hand); Menachot 27a (mutually inclusive).')
# ---- piska 124 (19:5-10): work invalidates to the ashes; the burnt bulls excluded; the dung's place; bits returned; the wood; the three species by "taking"; the hyssop; into the burning; the caster, the burner, the gatherer; the immersion in forty se'ah; the clean man; the ashes' three parts; the clean place; "in keeping"; the cow that drank ----
srow(124, 1, 'MATERIAL', 'WORK INVALIDATES FROM THE SLAUGHTER TO THE ASHES; "BEFORE HIS EYES" — NOT THE BURNT BULLS (THE A-FORTIORIS BOTH WAYS, THE VERSES DECIDING); ANOTHER BURNS, ELEAZAR LOOKS ON; THE DUNG IN ITS PLACE — THE BLOOD RETURNED OR THE HEIFER INVALID; BITS OF FLESH RETURNED, BONE NOT; MOST OF IT CONSUMED (YISHMAEL) OR THE WOOD NOT DIMINISHED (YEHUDAH); "TAKING" HERE AND AT 19:18 — THREE SPECIES BOTH; A CHIP OF CEDAR; HYSSOP WITHOUT AN EPITHET; SCARLET BY ITS NAME; INTO THE BURNING WHEN MOST HAS CAUGHT (OR WHEN IT SPLITS, AKIVA); THE CASTER, THE BURNER AND THE GATHERER ALL UNCLEAN TO GARMENTS AND BARRED FROM THE CAMP; NOT PLAGUE-GARMENTS; "IN WATER" TWICE — HANDS AND VESSELS IN A MAN\'S FORTY SE\'AH; "A CLEAN MAN" — ANY MAN, NOT A PRIEST; THE MINOR OUT, THE WOMAN IN (YISHMAEL) OR THE STRANGER IN, THE WOMAN BY "CLEAN", THE MINOR OUT BY "PLACE" (AKIVA); CLEAN FOR THE TITHE, UNCLEAN FOR TERUMAH — THE TVUL YOM; ASHES NOT BRANDS; THREE PARTS; THE CLEAN PLACE — A LIDDED VESSEL IN THE TENT OF THE DEAD IS UNCLEAN; "IN KEEPING FOR WATERS OF SPRINKLING" — WORK INVALIDATES THE WATER TOO, UNTIL IT IS SPRINKLING WATER; THE COW THAT DRANK IT — THIRTY-TWO ELDERS, R. YOSSI HAGELILI\'S RETURN, AND R. TARFON\'S DANIEL. "And he shall burn the heifer before his eyes" (19:5): work invalidates the BURNING — the a-fortiori from the slaughter would give it, so the verse teaches: from the slaughter UNTIL IT BECOMES ASHES (the whole rite one act); "before HIS eyes" — not the bulls burnt outside (Yom Kippur\'s, the anointed priest\'s): the a-fortiori (the bulls processed within, the more) REFUTED (their slaughter is not invalidated by work), the counter-a-fortiori on their slaughter REFUTED by "the heifer" and "before him" — the two verses decide (I1 both ways, the ink closing it); "another burns and Eleazar looks on"; "its skin, its flesh, its blood on its dung" — as the dung stays in its place so all: any blood left in his hand RETURNED to its place or THE HEIFER INVALID — he wipes his hand on the heifer\'s body (the ink: the burn-list is the sin-bull\'s of Exod 29:14, Lev 4:11, 16:27 WITH THE BLOOD ADDED — computed); "and he shall burn" — bits leaping from the fire: flesh returned (else invalid), bone not ("bone" unwritten), an olive-size returned; "he shall burn" after "and he shall burn" — amplification after amplification DIMINISHES: most of it consumed suffices (R. Yishmael; I8\'s cousin the tradition\'s own rule on doubled words), R. YEHUDAH: do not diminish the wood — bundles of hyssop and Grecian hyssop added to increase the ashes; "and the priest shall take cedar wood, hyssop and scarlet" (19:6) — "taking" here and "taking" at 19:18: THREE SPECIES THERE TOO though hyssop alone is written (I2 — the sprinkling\'s bundle carried by the word); "wood" any / "cedar" even a branch — a CHIP of cedar; "hyssop" — not Grecian, Kochalith, desert or Roman: NO EPITHET (the species by its bare name — a data rule on the noun; the ink: hyssop PLENE at Passover, the heifer and Psalm 51, DEFECTIVE at the leper\'s five, computed); "ushni tola\'ath" — "whose variant is tola\'ath": scarlet (the ink: the leper\'s word order, computed); "into the midst of the burning of the heifer" — not when ashes ("the heifer"), not before it burns ("the burning"): when the flames hold MOST of it; R. AKIVA: when it SPLITS of itself (two readings by two words, I13); "and the priest shall wash his garments and bathe his flesh in water" (19:7) — the caster of the hyssop imparts tumah to garments; "and then he shall come to the camp" — as the caster is barred until cleansed, so the burner and the gatherer; "unclean until the evening" — so the burner of Yom Kippur\'s bull and goat (Lev 16:26-28); "and he who burns it" (19:8) — the burner too (the a-fortiori from the caster would give it: the verse teaches that ALL who handle the heifer from beginning to end wash, bathe and wait for sunset); "and he who burns IT" — not plague-garments\' burner (the a-fortiori from contact refuted by the clause — I1 capped); "in water... in water" TWICE — a man immerses in what suffices for him (forty se\'ah), and the doubling puts HANDS AND VESSELS in the same measure (the data channel\'s forty, its verse the doubled word — Mishnah Mikvaot); "and a clean MAN shall gather" (19:9) — not a priest: ANY man (the gathering kasher through all — against the rite\'s priests); R. YISHMAEL: "a man" excludes the minor, "clean" includes the woman; R. AKIVA: "a man" includes the STRANGER, "clean" the woman, and "he shall PLACE it" excludes the minor (no mind to place) — two readings, the same three verdicts (Mishnah Parah 5:4; the ink: "a clean man" at 19:9 and 19:18 alone, computed); "a clean man" — clean for the TITHE though unclean for TERUMAH: the TVUL YOM (one who immersed by day) — R. Akiva\'s a-fortiori from the gatherer to the sprinkler (19:18) refused: "clean" there is "one who has LEFT the category of tumah", the tvul yom, and "clean" at 19:19 the same (I2 across the three seats): THE HEIFER PROCESSED BY A TVUL YOM — the Sadducees\' dispute the answer sheet (Parah 3:7); "the ashes" — not the brands: a brand with ash crushed, without ash discarded, a bone always crushed; "outside the camp" — the Mount of Olives, divided in THREE: the chel, the Mount, the priestly watches (the data channel\'s partition); "in a clean place" — its surroundings clean: R. ELAZAR HAKAPPAR — a lidded vessel of the ashes in the tent of the dead is UNCLEAN, "in a clean place" (the lid rule of 19:15 overridden for the ashes by the place-word); "and it shall be for the congregation in KEEPING for waters of sprinkling" — WORK invalidates the water too (not the heifer alone), but only until it IS sprinkling water ("for" = to make them); THE COW THAT DRANK THE WATER — its flesh unclean within a day? R. YEHUDAH: nullified in its bowels — "in keeping": once not in keeping, no tumah: asked before THIRTY-TWO ELDERS, ruled clean; R. YOSSI HAGELILI against R. AKIVA, dismissed, then substantiating from "in keeping" and asking "may I return?" — "shall I let everyone return and not you?"; R. TARFON reading DANIEL 8:4-7 on it — the ram R. Akiva, the goat R. Yossi, the horns broken, "none to rescue" the thirty-two elders: THE ACADEMY\'S OWN DISPUTE RECORDED AS A VISION\'S FULFILMENT (the shelf\'s aggadah (the lore, not the law) on a law seat, verdicted with the law). The exam: Mishnah Parah 3:9-11, 4:1-4 (work, the burning, the species), 5:4 (the gatherer), 9:5 (the cow that drank), 3:7 (the tvul yom); Mikvaot 1-2 (the forty); Yoma 2a, 8a-b (the seven days); Zevachim 93a.')
srow(124, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"IT IS A SIN-OFFERING" — ME\'ILAH OBTAINS (BUT NOT FOR ITS ASHES); UNFIT IF NOT FOR ITS NAME; NOT BURNED AT NIGHT; NOT VOIDED BY OVERNIGHT — "IN KEEPING" DAY AFTER DAY, YEAR AFTER YEAR; THE GATHERER UNCLEAN BY MOVING (HESSET); THE PROSELYTE INCLUDED; THE GENERATIONS. "It is a sin-offering" (19:9): ME\'ILAH (trespass) obtains for it as for a sin-offering; "it" — the heifer, not its ASHES; variantly: slaughtered not for its name — unfit; variantly: not burned at night as a sin-offering is not; and "it is a sin-offering" would void it by overnight keeping — "in keeping" answers: it KEEPS day after day and year after year (the heifer is a sin-offering for me\'ilah, intent and daytime, and not for overnight — the clause\'s three jobs and its one bar, I13\'s pattern; the ink: "it is a sin-offering" the burnt bull\'s formula of Exod 29:14, computed); "and he who gathers the ashes shall wash his garments" (19:10) — the gatherer imparts tumah to garments; the a-fortiori from the burner would give it — the verse ADDS: unclean by HESSET, by moving the ashes without touching; "for the children of Israel and for the sojourner" — the proselytes included because the command is to Israel; "an everlasting statute" — the generations. The exam: Mishnah Me\'ilah 3:1? (the heifer and its ashes); Menachot 51b-52a (me\'ilah on the heifer); Chullin 11a; Parah 4:1 (not for its name).')
# ---- piska 125 (19:11-13): the dead by contact; the eight-month birth and its blood; seven days; "with it"; the third and the seventh — the second sprinkling not optional; the punishment is uncleanness, not karet; "that shall die" — the sheretz by the a-fortiori and its return; "not cleansed" by blood — the lacking-atonement; the mishkan and the mikdash; karet and death one; other tumahs; the four failures ----
srow(125, 1, 'MATERIAL', 'THE DEAD CONFERS TUMAH BY CONTACT — THE VERSE INCLUDES THE EIGHT-MONTH BIRTH; ITS BLOOD OUT (YISHMAEL) OR IN (AKIVA); SEVEN DAYS; "WITH IT" — ASHES PROCESSED AS PRESCRIBED; THE THIRD AND THE SEVENTH — NOT "IF ON THE THIRD, CLEAN ON THE SEVENTH": 19:19 REPEATS TO VOID IT; THE PUNISHMENT FOR NOT SPRINKLING IS UNCLEANNESS, NOT KARET; "THAT SHALL DIE" — NOT UNTIL DEAD, AND THE SHERETZ BY THE A-FORTIORI, TRANSPOSED, RETURNED; "NOT CLEANSED" — BY BLOOD, THE LACKING-ATONEMENT (REBBI); THE MISHKAN AND THE MIKDASH BOTH; KARET AND DEATH ONE; "UNCLEAN SHALL HE BE" — THE OTHER TUMAHS (YOSHIYAH) OR THE FOUR FAILURES OF THE RITE (YONATHAN). "One who touches the dead of any human soul shall be unclean seven days" (19:11): the dead confers tumah by CONTACT — the a-fortiori from the tent would give it, so the verse INCLUDES the eight-month birth; and its BLOOD? "the soul of a man" EXCLUDES the blood (R. Yishmael) / "ALL the soul of a man" INCLUDES it (R. Akiva) — the same clause read narrow and wide (I4 against I5 on one phrase; the ink: "a human soul" the blasphemer chapter\'s murder clause, Lev 24:17, and this — computed); "seven days" — the dead\'s tumah SEVEN, against contact\'s one; "he shall be cleansed WITH IT" (19:12) — with ashes processed as prescribed; "on the third day and on the seventh day" — BOTH sprinklings: not "if sprinkled on the third he is clean on the seventh" — 19:12\'s "if he is not cleansed on the third he shall not be clean on the seventh" might still read the seventh as the result of the third — so 19:19 "and he shall cleanse him ON THE SEVENTH DAY" REPEATS to void that reading (I13: the third verse fixing the schedule — the compile\'s two timers, both required); "and if he is not cleansed on the third" — why written? because 19:20\'s karet might be for not sprinkling — no: HIS PUNISHMENT IS NOT BEING CLEAN, NOT KARET (the karet is for defiling the sanctuary; the ink: "cut off" at 19:13 and 19:20, computed — the verdict\'s two effects separated: uncleanness for the omission, karet for the entry); "everyone who touches the dead in the soul of a man" (19:13) — the eight-month birth again; "THAT SHALL DIE" — no tumah until he DIES: from it the SHERETZ (a creeping thing) — the graver (the dead) confers none until dead, the lighter the less; TRANSPOSED (the sheretz confers while convulsing, so the man the more) — "that shall die" nullifies the transposition and the original stands (the protocol again — I1 with reversal; Mishnah Oholot 1:6 the answer sheet: none confer until the soul departs); "and he not be cleansed" — REBBI: by BLOOD — the LACKING-ATONEMENT (the zav, the leper, whose offering is unbrought) entering the sanctuary; not by the waters, since "the waters were not sprinkled" already says the waters (each clause given its own referent — I13); "that soul shall be cut off" — why here and at 19:20? there "the MIKDASH of the LORD" (the Temple), here "the MISHKAN" (the desert tabernacle) — BOTH (the ink: the two nouns at the two seats, computed — the Sifrei\'s pair measured); "cut off" here and "that they not DIE in their tumah" (Lev 15:31) — KARET AND DEATH ONE AND THE SAME (I2 on the two punishments); "unclean shall he be" — the other tumahs (sheretz, zav) included: R. YOSHIYAH; R. YONATHAN: not needed — Lev 15:31 already binds all tumahs, and the dead was ISOLATED for death and an offering to teach the others (I8 — the exemplar departing from the general to teach the general); so "unclean shall he be" = THE FOUR FAILURES: sprinkled not at all; sprinkled on the third, not the seventh — "unclean shall he be"; sprinkled twice, not immersed — "his tumah is upon him"; sprinkled and immersed, no sunset — "his tumah is STILL upon him" (the ink: "still his uncleanness is on him" one seat, computed): THE RITE\'S STATE MACHINE READ OFF THREE CLAUSES — the compile\'s four states. The exam: Mishnah Oholot 1:6 (until the soul departs), 7:4 (the eight-month birth); Parah 12:? (the sprinklings); Mishnah Keritot 1:1 (karet for entering unclean); Shevuot 1:4-6 (the atonement for the sanctuary\'s defilement); Yevamot 74b (the lacking-atonement); Chullin 72a (the blood).')
# ---- piska 126 (19:14-15): the tent — a new tumah; died outside and brought in; all tents; partially and entirely; the floor to the depths; the entrance not the sides — the grave by the a-fortiori and its return; evening tumah at the third remove; what is in the tent — straw excluded, the four vessels, metal, earthenware; whatever is cleansed is unclean; the open vessel — earthenware (Yoshiyah / Yonathan / Eliezer); "open" any amount; "upon" its opening; tzamid and patil; a vessel upon a lid not protected; the lidded vessels of ordure, stone and soil; the sheretz ----
srow(126, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"THIS IS THE TORAH" — A NEW TUMAH, THE TENT; DIED OUTSIDE AND BROUGHT IN (ISSI B. AKAVYA, OR THE A-FORTIORI); ALL THAT "TENTS" — FROM THE LEPER\'S TENTS (R. YITZCHAK); ENTERS PARTIALLY, IS IN IT ENTIRELY — THE FLOOR TO THE DEPTHS (R. ACHI); TUMAH FROM THE ENTRANCE, NOT THE OPEN SIDES — AND THE GRAVE BY THE A-FORTIORI, TRANSPOSED, RETURNED; EVENING TUMAH FROM THE SIDES BY THE THIRD REMOVE; "ALL THAT IS IN THE TENT" — NOT STRAW, TWIGS, WOOD AND STONES: THE FOUR VESSELS OF THE MIDIAN SPOIL, THE METAL, THE EARTHENWARE; WHATEVER IS SUBJECT TO CLEANSING IS SUBJECT TO TUMAH; "EVERY OPEN VESSEL" — EARTHENWARE (YOSHIYAH, FROM THE SHERETZ\'S FOUR; YONATHAN, FROM "OPEN"; ELIEZER, FROM "UNCLEAN" FOREVER); "OPEN" ANY AMOUNT; "UPON IT" — ITS OPENING; TZAMID THE STOPPER, PATIL THE LID — BAAL PEOR\'S "ADHERED"; PROTECTED BY A LID IN THE TENT OF THE DEAD, BY A COVERING IN PLAGUE-TENTS; A LID UPON IT, NOT IT UPON A LID; ORDURE, STONE AND SOIL VESSELS PROTECT THE MORE; WHAT PROTECTS IN THE TENT PROTECTS ITSELF FROM THE SHERETZ. "This is the Torah: a man who dies in a tent" (19:14) — A NEW TUMAH: the dead effects TENT-uncleanness; died outside and brought in? "this is the Torah" — one law for both (Issi b. Akavya); R. YISHMAEL: not needed — if he effects it when not unclean before, the more when already unclean (I1); all things that "tent" are tents (not flax alone): R. YITZCHAK from the leper\'s tents, the lighter (I1 — the ink: "a man who dies in a tent" one seat; the tent rendered by the TABERNACLE-word in Onkelos, one Aramaic noun with 19:13\'s tabernacle of the LORD, computed); "all that enter the tent" — PARTIALLY; "all that is in the tent" — ENTIRELY (the a-fortiori would give it — R. ACHI: "all that is in the tent" renders THE FLOOR TO THE DEPTHS as the house); the tent imparts tumah from its ENTRANCE, not from its open sides — from it the GRAVE by the a-fortiori (a tent susceptible of tumah gives none from its sides; a grave not susceptible the less), TRANSPOSED (the grave gives from all sides, so the tent the more), the transposition nullified by "all that enter the tent" — "I have reasoned a fortiori and transposed; the transposition is nullified and I return" (the formula a third time in one portion); but evening tumah from the sides — by the a-fortiori from the third remove (19:22): the second remove the more; "and all that is in the tent shall be unclean" — straw, twigs, wood and stones? 19:18 "upon all the VESSELS" — vessels of ordure, earth and soil? Num 31:20 "every garment, every vessel of skin, every work of goats, every vessel of wood" — FOUR; metal by 31:22 "the silver and the gold"; earthenware by 19:15 "every open vessel" — SIX CLASSES from three verses; but perhaps those are subject to cleansing and straw to tumah alone? 19:11-12 "unclean seven days; he shall be cleansed with it" — WHATEVER IS SUBJECT TO CLEANSING IS SUBJECT TO TUMAH, and only that (the vessel census the Midian war\'s own list — the heifer\'s RUN at chapter 31 cited for its data; the ink: 31:19-24 run the third-and-seventh schedule and the waters of niddah, computed); "and every open vessel whose cover is not fastened upon it is unclean" (19:15) — EARTHENWARE: R. YOSHIYAH from the sheretz\'s four vessels with one excluded for both leniency and stringency (Lev 11:33), so here (I3); R. YONATHAN from "open" — a vessel unclean at the atmosphere of its opening, not its outside (the earthenware\'s own rule); R. ELIEZER from "it is unclean" — forever, no cleansing (earthenware\'s); "open" — any amount; ABBA CHANAN in R. Eliezer\'s name: "upon IT" — upon its opening, not upon all of it; "tzamid patil" — TZAMID the stopper, PATIL the lid — the allusion Num 25:3 "and Israel ADHERED (vayitzamed) to Baal Peor" (the ink: the lid\'s two words are Rebekah\'s BRACELETS, Gen 24:22, and the frontplate\'s and the fringe\'s CORD — computed; the translation inserts "of earthenware", cut); vessels protected in the tent of the dead by a tzamid patil, in plague-tents by a covering; "a tzamid patil UPON IT" — not it upon a tzamid patil: a jug inverted and smeared at its sides is susceptible (R. Eliezer; Mishnah Kelim 10:2); vessels of ordure, stone and soil PROTECT by a lid the more (the a-fortiori from earthenware — I1); "IT is unclean" — what protects by a lid in the tent of the dead protects ITSELF from the sheretz by a lid. The exam: Mishnah Oholot 1-3 (the tent, the grave, the entrance), 5:5-6 (stone and ordure vessels), 15 (the sides); Kelim 2:1, 9:8-10:8 (earthenware, the lid — tzamid patil); Shabbat 84a; Chullin 25a (open vessels); Eduyot 1:14.')
# ---- piska 127 (19:16-17): the open field — the fetus (Yishmael) or the coffin's lid and frame (Akiva); the sword unclean seven days — vessels and man, and vessels-man-vessels from 31:24; the dead removed from "slain" to equate what separates from it (Yoshiyah) or by the a-fortiori from neveilah (Yonathan) with the carrying rule; the killer as the toucher (Meir); the bone of a man — a limb from the living, the barley-corn bone; flesh, sinews, bones; the closed grave and the derivation from a derivation; "earth" not "ashes" — the sotah's identity ----
srow(127, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"ON THE OPEN FIELD" — THE DEAD FETUS IN ITS MOTHER (YISHMAEL) OR THE COFFIN\'S LID AND FRAME (AKIVA); THE SWORD UNCLEAN SEVEN DAYS AS ITS TOUCHER; VESSELS-MAN-VESSELS FROM THE MIDIAN GARMENTS (31:24); "SLAIN" AND "DEAD" — THE DEAD REMOVED FROM THE CATEGORY TO EQUATE AN OLIVE OF FLESH WITH THE BODY (YOSHIYAH); OR BY THE A-FORTIORI FROM NEVEILAH, ITS REFUTATION ANSWERED — AND CARRYING BY THE SAME (YONATHAN); THE KILLER EQUATED WITH THE TOUCHER — BY CONNECTION (MEIR). "And all who touch on the open field" (19:16): to include the DEAD FETUS in its mother\'s body (R. Yishmael) / the coffin\'s TOP-LID AND FRAME (R. Akiva) — the field-clause read to two absent cases (the ink: "on the open field" the leper\'s bird\'s clause, Lev 14:7, among its eleven — computed); "one slain by the sword" — THE SWORD is unclean seven days, as its toucher: VESSELS AND MAN; and vessels-man-VESSELS? Num 31:24 "and you shall wash your GARMENTS on the seventh day" — the garments are vessels (the Midian war\'s verse the source for the third remove — the run read as the spec\'s continuation); "slain by the sword or a dead body" — R. YOSHIYAH: the dead was in the category of "slain" and REMOVED for special mention to equate what SEPARATES from it (an olive of flesh) with the body (I8\'s form); R. YONATHAN: the dead is no member of "slain" (each named alone) — then what separates equated by the a-fortiori from NEVEILAH (the lesser; Lev 11:39-40), REFUTED (neveilah\'s tumah is the common evening one, the dead\'s the rare seven-day), the refutation ANSWERED (where is the tumah graver? in the dead\'s seven days); and CARRYING by the a-fortiori from neveilah (which confers by carrying), with its own refutation (then evening only?) answered — where touching gives seven, carrying gives seven; where touching gives evening, carrying gives evening (I1 with the tumah-length as the ratio\'s guard); R. MEIR: not needed — Num 31:19 "whoever has killed a man, and whoever has touched a slain one" EQUATES THE KILLER WITH THE TOUCHER: by connection — one who shot an arrow and killed is not unclean (the killing by contact the datum; the ink: "one slain by the sword" the singular\'s ONE seat in the Bible, Ezekiel\'s eleven the plural — computed). The exam: Mishnah Oholot 1:1-3 (the sword, the removes), 2:1-3 (the olive of flesh, the fetus), 7:4; Eduyot 6:3; Chullin 71b-72b (the fetus, the sword); Nazir 53b-54a (the sword and the slain); Pesachim 14b.')
srow(127, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"OR THE BONE OF A MAN" — A LIMB FROM THE LIVING; THE BARLEY-CORN BONE IS 19:18\'S "BONE". "Or the bone of a man" (19:16): a LIMB cut from a living person — the barley-corn bone? 19:18\'s "him who touched a bone" already speaks of the barley-corn: TWO BONES at two seats — the limb from the living at 19:16, the barley-corn at 19:18 (I13\'s form: two clauses, two referents; the ink: the four sources REORDERED between the touching verse and the sprinkling verse, the bone moved to the head — computed; "a human bone" with Gog\'s burial, Ezek 39:15). The exam: Mishnah Oholot 1:7-8, 2:1 (the barley-corn bone, the limb); Eduyot 6:2-3 (the limb from the living); Chullin 128b-129b.')
srow(127, 3, 'MATERIAL', '"A DEAD BODY OR THE BONE OF A MAN" — THE LIMB AS THE BODY: FLESH, SINEWS AND BONES. As the dead is flesh, sinews and bones, so the limb from the living — in its natural state (I3, the dead the paradigm for the limb; Mishnah Oholot 1:8\'s two hundred and forty-eight limbs the answer sheet\'s count — a data row on the body).')
srow(127, 4, 'MATERIAL', '"OR A GRAVE" — A CLOSED GRAVE; THE OPEN GRAVE\'S SIDES BY THE A-FORTIORI FROM THE TENT — AND "A DERIVATION FROM A DERIVATION?" REFUSED. "Or a grave" (19:16): a CLOSED grave (less than a handbreadth between the body and the lid); the open grave? the a-fortiori from the tent (susceptible, gives none from its open sides — the grave the less); but as the open tent gives EVENING tumah from behind, so the grave? — the tent\'s evening tumah is itself derived (126:1\'s a-fortiori from the third remove): "WOULD YOU DERIVE SOMETHING WHICH IS ITSELF DERIVED — A DERIVATION FROM A DERIVATION?" — the bar on second-hand paradigms stated as a question (the Korach sitting\'s 118:1 "you would learn from what is itself learned?" — the same governance rule, its second seat in the walk; MIDDOT.md). The exam: Mishnah Oholot 15:8-9 (the closed grave, the handbreadth), 3:7; Berakhot 19b (the grave\'s sides).')
srow(127, 5, 'MATERIAL', '"FROM THE EARTH OF THE BURNING" — NOT "ASHES": THE IDENTITY WITH THE SOTAH\'S EARTH (R. SHIMON) — EARTH ON WATER BOTH, THE ORDER, AND ASHES TURNED EARTH STILL VALID. "And they shall take for the unclean one from the EARTH of the burning" (19:17): R. SHIMON — is it earth? is it not ASHES? Scripture departs from its meaning to FORMULATE AN IDENTITY: "earth" here, "earth" at the sotah (5:17 "from the earth on the floor of the tabernacle... into the water") — as there the earth goes ON THE WATER, so here; as there the earth taken before the water is valid, so here (I2 — the gezerah shavah\'s license the departed word: the ink: ASHES at 19:9-10, DUST at 19:17, and the sotah\'s dust at 5:17 — computed, the Sifrei\'s reading measured on the tokens); variantly: ashes that changed to earth\'s appearance are valid. The exam: Mishnah Parah 6:1-3 (the order of ashes and water, the sotah likeness); Sotah 16b (the identity); Temurah 12b.')
# ---- piska 128 (19:17-18): living waters — spring waters, Isaac's well; "in a vessel" — all vessels as earthenware, against the sotah; "taking" — three species; hyssop without epithet ----
srow(128, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"LIVING WATERS" — SPRING WATER, ISAAC\'S WELL THE SUPPORT; "IN A VESSEL" — ALL VESSELS EQUATED WITH EARTHENWARE, AGAINST THE SOTAH\'S. "And he shall place upon it living waters in a vessel" (19:17): SPRING waters — or "waters which are life to the world"? support in Gen 26:19 "a well of LIVING waters" dug by Isaac\'s servants (the ink: "living waters" at Isaac\'s well, the leper\'s bird, the heifer and the Prophets\' eight, computed; Onkelos "SPRING water" — the Sifrei\'s arm the translation\'s text, cut); "in a vessel" — ALL VESSELS equated with earthenware: water and earth are consecrated at the sotah and at the heifer, and the sotah\'s vessel is earthenware alone (5:17) — "in a vessel" unqualified here breaks the likeness (I3 refused by the ink\'s own bare noun; the ink: the vessel-word the leper\'s Lev 14:5, 14:50, computed). The exam: Mishnah Parah 8:8-11 (which waters are living — the rivers, the sea, the Jordan disqualified), 5:5 (all vessels); Mikvaot 1:8.')
srow(128, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"A CLEAN MAN SHALL TAKE" — "TAKING" AS AT 19:6, THREE SPECIES; HYSSOP WITHOUT AN EPITHET. "Taking" here (19:18) and "taking" at 19:6 (the export: "Ibid. 5") — as there three objects, so here (I2; the bundle for the sprinkling the same three: cedar, hyssop, scarlet); "hyssop" — not Greek, Kochalith, Roman or desert hyssop: none with an epithet (124:1\'s rule at its second seat; Mishnah Parah 11:7). The exam: Parah 11:7-9 (the hyssop\'s kinds, three stalks, the dipping).')
# ---- piska 129 (19:18-21): enough water for dipping; the minor out, the woman in / clean of all tumah — the tvul yom, and clean here as at the gatherer; the tent susceptible; only what was there; the barley-corn bone; the four for sprinkling as for tumah; the clean sprinkler unclean (Akiva) or only the unclean things (the sages); the third and seventh — the seventh repeated; washing on the eighth and ninth; karet for the sanctuary, not for the sprinkling; the generations; the sprinkler's water-measure ----
srow(129, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"A CLEAN MAN SHALL DIP IN THE WATER" — ENOUGH WATER TO DIP THREE STALKS; "A MAN" NOT A MINOR, "CLEAN" A WOMAN (YISHMAEL); "CLEAN" OF ALL TUMAH — THE TVUL YOM EXCLUDED? NO: "CLEAN" HERE AS "CLEAN" AT THE GATHERER — UNCLEAN FOR THE SIN-OFFERING BOTH (AKIVA); THE TENT SUSCEPTIBLE; ONLY WHAT WAS THERE WHEN IT BECAME UNCLEAN; THE BARLEY-CORN BONE; THE FOUR SOURCES FOR SPRINKLING AS FOR TUMAH. "And a clean man shall dip in the water" (19:18): enough water for DIPPING — three calyxes (the data channel: three stalks of hyssop, Mishnah Parah 11:9); "a man" — not a minor; "clean" — a woman included (R. Yishmael, 124:1\'s reading at the sprinkler); R. AKIVA: "clean" not needed for a woman (the a-fortiori from the gatherer) — "clean" = CLEAN OF ALL TUMAH, excluding the tvul yom? — and then: "clean" here and "clean" at 19:9 — as "clean" there is unclean vis-a-vis the sin-offering, so here (I2 — the tvul yom readmitted: the two readings of the same word set side by side, the second by the identity; the ink: "a clean man" at 19:9 and 19:18 alone, computed); "and he shall sprinkle it upon the tent" — the TENT is susceptible of tumah; variantly: only vessels THERE when the tent became unclean require sprinkling, not those brought in after the body was removed (a timing rule read off "the tent"); "upon him who touched a bone" — the barley-corn bone (127:2\'s pair); "a slain one, a dead body, a grave" — all four for SPRINKLING as all four for TUMAH (the ink: the same four nouns, REORDERED — computed). The exam: Mishnah Parah 12:1-2 (the dipping, the three stalks), 5:4, 12:10 (who sprinkles — the woman, the minor); Yevamot 72b-73a (the tvul yom); Oholot 1:1.')
srow(129, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"THE CLEAN SPRINKLES ON THE UNCLEAN" — THE CLEAN SPRINKLER BECOMES UNCLEAN (AKIVA) OR ONLY UNCLEAN THINGS ARE SPRINKLED (THE SAGES); THE THIRD AND SEVENTH REQUIRED — "ON THE SEVENTH" REPEATED TO VOID THE OTHER READING; WASHING ON THE EIGHTH AND NINTH TOO. "And the clean one shall sprinkle on the unclean one" (19:19): R. AKIVA — the clean one who sprinkles becomes UNCLEAN (the paradox the tradition owns — Mishnah Parah 12:5, Yoma 14a\'s "the clean unclean and the unclean clean"); THE SAGES: Scripture speaks only of things that HAVE become unclean as sprinkled — not of the clean (a dispute on the verse\'s subject and object; the ink: "the clean shall sprinkle on the unclean" one seat, computed); "on the third day and on the seventh day" — both required (125:1 again) — "and he shall cleanse him ON THE SEVENTH DAY" repeats to make the omission invalid; "and he shall wash his garments and bathe in water" — on the seventh only? the EIGHTH AND NINTH too: "he shall cleanse him on the seventh" followed by "and he shall wash... and be clean in the evening" — the washing may follow the seventh (the ink: "and he shall be clean in the evening" the chapter\'s one positive evening against eighteen "unclean until the evening", computed). The exam: Mishnah Parah 12:5-6 (the sprinkler), 3:? ; Yoma 14a (the sprinkler unclean); Mishnah Parah 12:11 (the sprinkling by day).')
srow(129, 3, 'MATERIAL', '"AND A MAN WHO BECOMES UNCLEAN AND DOES NOT PURIFY HIMSELF" — KARET FOR THE SANCTUARY, NOT FOR THE SPRINKLING. "And a man, if he becomes unclean and does not purify himself" (19:20): the DEFILEMENT OF THE SANCTUARY and its holy things — its punishment karet; for not sprinkling? 19:12 "if he does not purify himself on the third and the seventh he shall not be clean" — the punishment is uncleanness, not karet (125:1\'s split at its second seat; the ink: "cut off from the midst of the assembly" — Korach\'s phrase "perished from the midst of the assembly", 16:33, and this alone — computed). The exam: Mishnah Keritot 1:1 (karet for the sanctuary); Shevuot 1:4-2:5 (the unclean who entered — knowledge, the two goats).')
srow(129, 4, 'CONTEXT', '"AND IT SHALL BE FOR THEM FOR AN EVERLASTING STATUTE" (19:21) — observed throughout the generations (the ink: "for an everlasting statute" at 19:10 and 19:21, the Torah\'s eight seats, computed). No operator.')
srow(129, 5, 'MATERIAL', '"THE SPRINKLER OF THE WATERS SHALL WASH HIS CLOTHING" — THE DISTINCTION IS THE WATER\'S MEASURE: ENOUGH FOR SPRINKLING RENDERS A MAN UNCLEAN TO HIS GARMENTS, LESS RENDERS HIM UNCLEAN TO FOODS AND DRINKS; NOT SPRINKLER-AGAINST-TOUCHER, NOT CLEAN-AGAINST-UNCLEAN, NOT FIT-AGAINST-UNFIT — EACH REFUSED BY AN A-FORTIORI. "And the sprinkler of the waters of niddah shall wash his clothing" (19:21) against "and he who touches the waters shall be unclean until the evening": the distinction is the WATER\'S MEASURE — water sufficient for sprinkling renders the man unclean so as to render his GARMENTS unclean; insufficient water renders him unclean so as to render FOODS AND DRINKS unclean; the three rival readings — sprinkler against toucher, clean against unclean, fit against unfit — each REFUSED by an a-fortiori (the sprinkler who touches the more; the unclean the more; the unfit the more): "YOU MUST PERFORCE ACCEPT THE FIRST" — a verdict by elimination of the alternatives (I1 as the exclusion tool; the ink: "the sprinkler" the participle\'s one seat, "the toucher of the waters of niddah" one seat, the waters of niddah twice in the verse — computed). The exam: Mishnah Parah 12:5 (the sprinkler and the measure); Yoma 14a; Mishnah Kelim 1:2 (the waters of niddah\'s measure among the fathers of uncleanness).')
# ---- piska 130 (19:22): the removes — vessels-man, vessels-man-vessels, vessels-vessels and vessels-man by a-fortiori; a man touching the dead renders his neighbor unclean until evening only, not seven days; and not by hesset ----
srow(130, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"ALL THAT THE UNCLEAN ONE TOUCHES SHALL BE UNCLEAN" — THE REMOVES: THE SWORD (VESSELS) AND ITS TOUCHER (MAN) SEVEN DAYS; VESSELS-MAN-VESSELS FROM 31:24; VESSELS-VESSELS AND VESSELS-MAN BY THE A-FORTIORI; A MAN UNCLEAN BY THE DEAD RENDERS HIS NEIGHBOR UNCLEAN UNTIL EVENING — NOT SEVEN DAYS (THE A-FORTIORI FROM VESSELS REFUSED BY THE CLAUSE), AND NOT BY MOVING (THE A-FORTIORI FROM NEVEILAH REFUSED BY "TOUCHES"). "And all that the unclean one touches shall become unclean" (19:22): the chain — the sword unclean seven days and its toucher (19:16: vessels and man); vessels-man-vessels from Num 31:24\'s garments; vessels-VESSELS by the a-fortiori (vessels that touch a man who touched vessels are unclean — vessels touching vessels the more); vessels-MAN likewise; but a man unclean by the dead rendering his NEIGHBOR unclean seven days — the a-fortiori from vessels (not rendered unclean by bed and seat, yet conferring seven; a man, rendered unclean by bed and seat, the more) is REFUSED by the clause: "and the soul that touches him shall be unclean UNTIL THE EVENING" — evening, not seven (I1 capped by the ink); and by HESSET (moving)? the a-fortiori from neveilah (the lesser, which renders by moving) refused by "TOUCHES" — no tumah by moving: THE REMOVES TABLE built from two clauses, two verses of chapter 31 and two capped a-fortioris (Mishnah Oholot 1:1-3\'s rows — the compile\'s remove-count as data; the ink: the chapter\'s eighteen "until the evening" and the chain\'s two clauses — computed). The exam: Mishnah Oholot 1:1-3 (the four removes; the man and the vessels); Pesachim 14b, 17a-19b (the removes); Nazir 42b-43a; Chullin 124b.')
